       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 30 mg is three times the maximum recommended daily dose of amlodipine, which is 10 mg once daily for adults. Higher doses raise the risk of severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema, and they don't add meaningful benefit.\n\nCould this be a typo or a mix-up? Some possibilities:\n- **Intended 10 mg daily**, the usual maximum.\n- **Intended 5 mg daily**, the usual starting dose. Elderly or frail patients often start at 2.5 mg.\n- **Intended 30 mg of a different drug**, such as nifedipine ER, which is commonly dosed at 30 mg.\n\nPlease check the intended dose with your supervising resident or attending. Once you confirm it, I'll enter the order right away. If a higher dose is deliberate, tell me the rationale, and I can add a note to the order. It will probably need attending sign-off and pharmacy verification."
      }
