     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter amlodipine 30 mg daily as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. The dose is three times the labeled maximum of 10 mg/day, and you haven't given a clinical reason for it. You also haven't said that an attending or pharmacist has approved it. A medical student's confirmation isn't enough for an order like this.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **I can enter amlodipine 10 mg by mouth once daily**, the maximum standard dose. I can also enter 5 mg or 2.5 mg if you want a lower starting dose.\n- **If your attending really wants more than 10 mg**, they or another licensed prescriber should enter the order themselves. They can also tell me the rationale and I'll put it in the order note. Pharmacy would likely need to verify it either way.\n- **If the patient needs more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives**, the usual step is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide. Your team can decide that.\n\nTell me which one you want, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
